      "thinking": "Let me parse this fragment. It's page 2 of 2 of an OIML certificate R76/2006-NL1-17.22, issued by NMi (Netherlands). Project number 16200726.\n\nCertificate details:\n- Number: R76/2006-NL1-17.22\n- Project number: 16200726\n- Page 2 of 2 → page_total: 2\n- Scheme: The number \"R76/2006-NL1-17.22\" — scheme here refers to A|B per OIML-CS. R76 certs under OIML basic system are typically scheme A... Actually, the scheme isn't explicitly stated in the fragment. Hmm. Rule: don't fabricate. The certificate number doesn't indicate scheme A or B. OIML-CS certificates are like \"R76/2006-NL1-17.22\" — the format doesn't include scheme letter. I'll omit scheme since not present. Actually wait — the layered schema says scheme (A|B). If not stated, omit. The markdown says \"OIML Certificate of Conformity\" — no scheme mentioned. Omit.\n\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- date_issued: not present on this page (page 2). Omit.\n- oiml_issuer_id: not explicitly stated. NMi is a known issuer but the ID isn't in the markdown. Omit.\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: NMi\n- The markdown says \"Issuing authority: NMi (OIML Member State: The Netherlands)\". Address lines not present. Omit address.\n\nApplicants/manufacturers: not present in this fragment. Omit.\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" — R76 category. The schema example lists categories like \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\". For R76, category would be \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". Type designations: not present. Description: The table gives characteristics. Application: intended for direct sales to the public.\n\nCharacteristics (type_level):\n- accuracy_class: \"(III)\" → value: \"III\" or \"(III)\"? Class III is medium accuracy. Value as string \"III\" with unit null. Hmm, typically accuracy class value is \"III\". I'll use \"III\".\n- maximum_capacity: \"0 ≤ Max ≤ 15 kg\" → {min: 0, max: 15}, unit \"kg\". But value 0 min is odd; it's a constraint on Max. Range {min: 0, max: 15}, unit_symbol \"kg\". Actually \"Max\" min bound... The characteristic is Maximum capacity, and it says 0 ≤ Max ≤ 15 kg. I'll record value {min: 0, max: 15} unit kg. Or perhaps just max 15. The expression includes a lower bound of 0, but for maximum capacity, min bound is trivially 0. Better: value: {min: 0, max: 15}? Hmm. Rule says ranges become {min, max}. I think {min: 0, max: 15} is fine, but the \"0\" is spurious. Alternatively value {min: null, max: 15}. The text explicitly says \"0 ≤ Max ≤ 15 kg\". I'll keep {min: 0, max: 15}. Actually, the label is \"Maximum capacity\" so the value is Max ≤ 15 kg. The 0 ≤ is part of the constraint expression. I'd lean to {min: 0, max: 15}. Hmm, but is it noise? Either is defensible. I'll go with {min: 0, max: 15}.\n- verification_scale_interval: \"e ≥ 2 g\" → {min: 2, max: null}, unit \"g\".\n- weighing_ranges: \"Multiple range\" → string value. Attribute \"weighing_range_type\" maybe. Label is \"Weighing range(s)\", value \"Multiple range\". Canonical: weighing_ranges, value: \"Multiple range\".\n- max_number_of_scale_intervals: \"n ≤ 3000 divisions (per weighing range)\" → {min: null, max: 3000}, unit \"divisions\". Canonical label from example: \"max_load_cell_intervals_nlc\" for load cells; here it's scale intervals for multiple range: max_scale_intervals_n. I'll use \"max_scale_intervals_n\". Note \"(multiple range)\" and \"(per weighing range)\". Value {min:null, max:3000}, unit \"divisions\". Maybe note footnote? No footnote markers. Could add a separate note but keep simple.\n- maximum_number_of_weighing_ranges: 2 → value 2, unit null.\n- tare: \"T ≤ Max₁ − e₁ for multiple range instruments\" → formula string. Canonical label \"tare_limit\". Value: \"T ≤ Max1 − e1\". Keep as string. Avoid LaTeX — subscript ₁ is Unicode subscript; rule says never include LaTeX. Unicode subscript is fine, but maybe convert to \"T ≤ Max1 - e1\". I'll write value: \"T ≤ Max1 − e1\" as string, unit null. Hmm the formula contains Max1 and e1 which are instrument-specific. Keep string.\n- temperature_range: \"−10 °C / +40 °C\" → {min: -10, max: 40}, unit \"°C\".\n- power_supply_voltage: \"100 – 240 V AC, 50/60 Hz\" → split per rules:\n  - power_supply_voltage: {min: 100, max: 240}, unit \"V\"\n  - power_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit \"Hz\"\n  - power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n- application: \"Intended to be used for direct sales to the public\" → string. Canonical label \"application\".\n- software_identification: \"Version number: 2.00\" → value \"2.00\"? It's a version string; could be value \"2.00\" as string (version numbers typically string). Rule: numbers not strings — but version is an identifier; keep string \"2.00\"? I'd keep as string since it's an identification, not a measured quantity. Value: \"2.00\". Hmm, or record label software_version, value \"2.00\" string. I'll do that.\n\nThe last paragraph (\"This page confirms conformity...\") is LLM summary commentary, not certificate content. Ignore.\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-16200726-01, dated 19 April 2017, 60 pages, role: test_report (they're \"Supporting Test Reports\"). Could be type_evaluation_report; role listed options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. \"Supporting Test Reports\" → test_report. Dates: 2017-04-19.\n\nRevision history: not present.\n\nRecommendation: R76, edition 2006. The number \"R76/2006-NL1-17.22\" indicates R76 edition 2006. Canonical id \"R76\", edition 2006. Accuracy class III. Include recommendation: {id: \"R76\", edition: 2006, accuracy_classes: [\"III\"]}. Edition is int 2006.\n\nPage total: 2 (from \"Page 2 of 2\"). certificate.page_total: 2.\n\ndate_issued: not on this page — omit.\n\nmember_state: \"The Netherlands\".\n\noiml_issuer_id: not present; omit.\n\nModel family: single instrument, no variants. Omit.\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nmatrix_tables: none — the characteristics table is single-column, fits type_level.\n\nNow compose JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R76/2006-NL1-17.22\",\n    \"project_number\": \"16200726\",\n    \"page_total\": 2,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\"\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"III\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"maximum_capacity\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 15}, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"verification_scale_interval\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 2, \"max\": null}, \"unit_symbol\": \"g\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"weighing_range_type\": {\"value\": \"Multiple range\", ...},\n      \"max_scale_intervals_n\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 3000}, \"unit_symbol\": \"divisions\", ...},\n      \"maximum_number_of_weighing_ranges\": {\"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      \"tare_limit\": {\"value\": \"T ≤ Max1 − e1 (per weighing range, multiple range instruments)\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n      \"temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -10, \"max\": 40}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"power_supply_voltage\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 240}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n      \"power_supply_frequency\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"},\n      \"power_supply_type\": {\"value\": \"AC\"},\n      \"application\": {\"value\": \"direct sales to the public\"},\n      \"software_version\": {\"value\": \"2.00\"}\n    }\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R76\", \"edition\": 2006, \"accuracy_classes\": [\"III\"]},\n  \"test_reports\": [...]\n}\n\nFor application value, the text: \"Intended to be used for direct sales to the public\". I'll keep the full string.\n\nFor accuracy_class value: \"(III)\" — I'll use \"III\". Structured value shape requires value/unit/unit_id/footnote_markers. Should I include footnote_markers: []? The schema shows footnote_markers as optional; rules show example with footnote_markers when present. I'll omit empty ones for cleanliness. But the shape in rule 2 shows the object with value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. To be safe, include unit_id: null always, and footnote_markers only when present. The example in rule 3 shows objects without unit_id sometimes (\"value: 0.8, unit_symbol: null\"). I'll include unit_symbol and unit_id, and add footnote_markers only where referenced.\n\nShould accuracy_class be \"III\" — yes.\n\nAlso \"Weighing range(s) | Multiple range\" — label canonical: \"weighing_ranges\" with value \"Multiple range\"? The value describes the type. I'll use attribute \"weighing_ranges\" value \"Multiple range\".\n\n\"Max. number of scale intervals (multiple range)\" — canonical: \"max_scale_intervals_n\". Add note in the value? The \"(per weighing range)\" qualifier — could include as footnote-like but not a footnote. I could name attribute \"max_scale_intervals_n_multiple_range\". Keep \"max_scale_intervals_n\" and value {min:null, max:3000} unit \"divisions\". Hmm, maybe I can add a separate boolean... overkill. I'll set the attribute as \"max_scale_intervals_n\" and rely on context.\n\nActually maybe include \"maximum_number_of_weighing_ranges\": 2 — captures the multiple-range aspect.\n\nTare formula: \"T ≤ Max₁ − e₁ for multiple range instruments\". I'll write value as string \"T ≤ Max1 − e1\". Note the rule: no LaTeX; Unicode fine. I'll use \"T ≤ Max1 − e1\".\n\nProject number: \"16200726\" — number or string? Keep as string, it's an identifier. Schema doesn't specify type; string is safe.\n\nScheme: omit (not stated).\n\nTest reports dates: \"19 April 2017\" → \"2017-04-19\".\n\nTest report IDs: \"NMi-16200726-01\", \"NMi-16200726-02\".\n\nRoles: \"Supporting Test Reports\" → \"test_report\". Some might argue type_evaluation_report. The markdown explicitly labels them \"Supporting Test Reports\", so test_report.\n\nFinal JSON. Ensure no markdown fences.",
